, 0.055267333984375, 0.0157623291015625, 0.00853729248046875, 0.01385498046875, 0.0053863525390625, 0.0251007080078125, 0.024993896484375, -0.0133819580078125, -0.020599365234375, 0.0187530517578125, 0.00939178466796875, 0.0178375244140625, -0.006267547607421875, -0.00707244873046875, -0.0244293212890625, 0.003086090087890625, 0.0216522216796875, 0.004638671875, 0.001522064208984375, 0.019317626953125, -0.00537872314453125, 0.01476287841796875, -0.023284912109375, -0.0014057159423828125, 0.004810333251953125, 0.0116729736328125, 0.022613525390625, 0.0194549560546875, -0.007598876953125, -0.00046133995056152344, 0.030426025390625, -0.0012950897216796875, 0.03125, -0.024505615234375, 0.0257568359375, 0.02191162109375, -0.036346435546875, 0.016937255859375, -0.007801055908203125, 0.0038356781005859375, 0.01430511474609375, -0.036041259765625, -0.00412750244140625, -0.007785797119140625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are essential for realizing low-carbon, centralized heat supply in urban areas. However, their operation and control remain complex, especially in systems with meshed topologies, nonlinear hydraulic and thermal dynamics, fluctuating renewable sources, and integrated thermal storage. Conventional optimization approaches\u2014such as mixed-integer nonlinear programming (MINLP) solvers and local, gradient-based methods\u2014struggle to effectively manage these complexities at large scales. Most published work emphasizes steady-state optimization or dynamic simulation, yet scalable global optimization for large, realistic, multi-period district heating networks is still an unresolved challenge, as confirmed by recent comprehensive reviews.\n\nTo address these gaps, we propose a comprehensive framework for scalable, high-fidelity global optimization of district heating networks. Below, we outline our objectives, summarize our methodological advancements, and provide an overview of our evaluation protocol.\n\n**Objectives and Main Contributions:**  \nThe primary aims of this work are as follows:\n- Develop a network modeling framework that explicitly represents both hydraulic and thermal nonlinearities.\n- Introduce algorithmic innovations enabling scalable global optimization on large, meshed networks with storage and multi-period operation.\n- Benchmark our methods systematically against standard MINLP and local optimization solvers, across diverse test scenarios.\n\nOur main contributions include:\n- A quantitative analysis and easily applicable checklist for when steady-state (rather than dynamic) modeling is appropriate, including supporting criteria.\n- New branch-and-bound and symmetry-exploiting search strategies, with summarized steps and a clarifying example (see below), targeted at large-scale topologies.\n- Release of reproducible synthetic and real-world datasets, together with randomization protocols and open-source code.\n- Performance analysis addressing uncertainties and practical limitations, plus practical guidance for model selection in operational contexts.\n\n**Overview of Modeling Framework:**  \nWe model the network at each discrete time step using a stationary, nonlinear formulation\u2014each seen as a \u201csteady-state snapshot.\u201d This approach is justified when changes in network state happen much slower than the selected time resolution. For example, if loads and sources transition on the scale of hours but the simulation time step is set to one hour or longer, this assumption holds well for most planning and operational tasks. In rapidly fluctuating scenarios or systems with very short time constants, fully dynamic models are preferable; we highlight quantitative limits below.\n\nThe following equations govern the network physics, with each variable defined at its first use:\n\n- **Hydraulics (Pressure-Flow):**  \n  The Darcy-Weisbach relation describes pressure drops in pipes:\n  \\[\n  \\Delta p_{ij} = f_{ij}(Q_{ij}, T_{ij}) = \\lambda_{ij} \\frac{L_{ij}}{D_{ij}} \\frac{1}{2} \\rho(T_{ij}) \\left( \\frac{Q_{ij}}{A_{ij}} \\right)^2\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\Delta p_{ij}\\): pressure drop across pipe \\((i,j)\\); \\(Q_{ij}\\): volumetric flow; \\(T_{ij}\\): average fluid temperature; \\(\\lambda_{ij}\\): (dimensionless) friction factor; \\(L_{ij}\\): pipe length; \\(D_{ij}\\): pipe diameter; \\(A_{ij} = \\pi D_{ij}^2 / 4\\): cross-sectional area; \\(\\rho(T)\\): temperature-dependent water density. We use cubic-polynomial empirical fits for \\(\\rho(T)\\) and heat capacity \\(c_p(T)\\) from tabulated water properties (see Appendix Sec. S1), which is critical for capturing temperature feedback effects in operational optimization.\n\n- **Thermal Energy Balances:**  \n  At each network node:\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{\\text{inlets}} \\dot{m}_{\\ell}^{\\text{in}} h(T_{\\ell}^{\\text{in}}) = \\sum_{\\text{outlets}} \\dot{m}_{m}^{\\text{out}} h(T_{m}^{\\text{out}}) + Q_{\\text{demand}, k}\n  \\]\n  Here, \\(\\dot{m}\\): mass flow rate; \\(h(T) = c_p(T) \\cdot T\\): specific enthalpy; \\(Q_{\\text{demand}, k}\\): nodal thermal demand; subscripts indicate inlets/outlets. Mixing of multiple inlet streams at a node uses enthalpy-weighted blending:\n  \\[\n  T_{\\text{mix}} = \\frac{\\sum_i \\dot{m}_i T_i}{\\sum_i \\dot{m}_i}\n  \\]\n  assuming \\(c_p(T)\\) varies only weakly near \\(T_{\\text{mix}}\\), appropriate for typical district heating water temperature ranges. This assignment ensures physically consistent node temperatures.\n\n- **Operational Limits:**  \n  At each time step, constraints are enforced on mass flow rates (\\(\\dot{m}_{\\min}, \\dot{m}_{\\max}\\)), pipe temperatures (\\(T_{\\min}, T_{\\max}\\)), and node pressures, based on equipment specifications and safe operating practices. Detailed parameter choices and units are included in Table S1. Users should avoid bounds that are excessively tight (causing overconstraint) or too loose (permitting physically implausible solutions).\n\n**Steady-State Assumption\u2014Quantitative Guidance and Model Selection:**  \nA central decision is whether steady-state or dynamic modeling should be used. Network inertia is characterized by two timescales:\n- **Hydraulic settling time** \\(\\tau_h = V_{\\text{net}} / \\dot{V}_{\\text{main}}\\), where \\(V_{\\text{net}}\\) is total network fluid volume (typically m\u00b3) and \\(\\dot{V}_{\\text{main}}\\) is the main flow rate (m\u00b3/s);\n- **Thermal propagation time** \\(\\tau_t = L_{\\text{max}} / v_{\\text{flow}}\\), where \\(L_{\\text{max}}\\) is the maximum path length from source to demand node and \\(v_{\\text{flow}}\\) is the typical flow velocity (m/s).\n\n**Example:**  \nLet \\(V_{\\text{net}} = 20{,}000\\,\\mathrm{m}^3\\), \\(\\dot{V}_{\\text{main}} = 1\\,\\mathrm{m}^3/\\mathrm{s}\\) \\(\\Rightarrow\\ \\tau_h = 20{,}000\\,\\mathrm{s} \\approx 5.5\\,\\mathrm{h}\\); and \\(L_{\\text{max}} = 5000\\,\\mathrm{m}\\), \\(v_{\\text{flow}} = 1\\,\\mathrm{m}/\\mathrm{s}\\) \\(\\Rightarrow\\ \\tau_t = 5000\\,\\mathrm{s} \\approx 1.4\\,\\mathrm{h}\\).\n\nFollowing best-practice guidance (see [3]: industry \u201crule-of-thumb\u201d analysis), if the simulation interval \\(\\Delta t \\geq 10 \\tau_h\\), steady-state errors are generally below 2%. In systems with frequent fast load changes (e.g. >40%/hr or with narrow, small-diameter pipes \\(D < 50\\,\\mathrm{mm}\\)), or with control decisions on minute-scales, dynamic models should be used\u2014steady-state simplification may then yield errors >10%. Users can follow this decision pathway:\n1. Calculate \\(\\tau_h\\) and \\(\\tau_t\\) for the network.\n2. Set or assess simulation time step \\(\\Delta t\\).\n3. If \\(\\Delta t \\geq 10 \\tau_h\\), steady-state is appropriate; otherwise, dynamic modeling is advised.\n4. Check if high-frequency/large-amplitude load variations are expected; if so, reconsider steady-state use.\nA summary checklist and a decision flowchart for model selection are in Table S2 and Fig. S1.\n\n**Algorithmic Methods and Illustrative Examples:**  \nTo make global optimization tractable for realistic district heating topologies, we implement several algorithmic improvements. While their greatest efficacy is on meshed, moderately sparse networks, their adaptation to highly irregular/dynamic layouts is reserved for future research. Each is explained below, now with illustrative examples for clarity:\n\n1. **Cyclic Flow Exclusion:**  \n   - *Purpose*: In a meshed network, flow around loops (cycles) must sum to zero due to conservation of mass (Kirchhoff's law), so some flow variables are redundant.  \n   - *Example*: In a triangle (nodes A\u2013B\u2013C\u2013A), only two of the three pipe flows are independent; the third is determined by loop closure. Paton\u2019s algorithm systematically finds such fundamental cycles. For each, we express one flow (e.g., \\(Q_{AB}\\)) as a linear combination of the others and remove it from optimization variables, reducing problem size (see Algorithm S1).\n\n2. **Supply-Return Symmetry Exploitation:**  \n   - *Purpose*: If the network includes symmetric (isomorphic) branches (e.g., identical consumer blocks), then swapping them does not affect the problem\u2019s outcome.  \n   - *Example*: If two consumer subnets are structurally identical, a graph automorphism tool such as NAUTY ([4]; which provides fast detection of such symmetries) labels these branches. During the branch-and-bound process, we can prune redundant solution trees, accelerating convergence.\n\n3. **Temperature Mixing Constraint Reduction:**  \n   - *Purpose*: Redundant constraints at mixing nodes increase computational overhead.  \n   - *Example*: For a node with three inlets, only two independent mixing equations are needed. Form the matrix of all mixing constraints, perform Gaussian elimination, and keep only the essential constraints to capture correct mixing (see Algorithm S2).\n\nTogether, these improvements\u2014demonstrated on an example mesh with 10 cycles and 4 symmetric subnetworks\u2014reduced the number of"}}